==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|ang|formest}}, {{m|ang|fyrmest||earliest, first, most prominent}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*frumistaz}}, from the locative stem {{m|gem-pro|*fur-}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fr-}} + the superlative suffix {{m|gem-pro|*-umistaz}}, stem ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pr-}}. The suffix {{m|gem-pro|*-umistaz}} was a compound suffix, created from the rarer comparative suffix {{m|gem-pro|*-umô}} (as in Old English {{m|ang|fruma}}) + the regular superlative suffix {{m|gem-pro|*-istaz}} (English {{m|en|-est}}); {{m|gem-pro|*-umô}} in turn is from {{der|en|ine-pro|*-mHo-}}.

Cognate with {{cog|ofs|formest}}, {{cog|got|𐍆𐍂𐌿𐌼𐌹𐍃𐍄𐍃}}. See {{m|en|for}}, {{m|en|first}} and Old English {{m|ang|fruma}} for more. Partially cognate to {{m|en|primus}}, from {{cog|ine-pro|*pr-}} + [[Latin]] superlative suffix {{m|la|-imus}}, from {{cog|ine-pro|*-mHo-}}.

A comparative {{m|en|former}} was back-formed analogically, leaving the ''m'' from {{m|gem-pro|*-umô}} in place. Later the Old English suffix complex {{m|ang||-(u)m-est}} was conflated with the word {{m|en|most}} through [[folk etymology]], so that the word is now interpreted as {{suffix|en|fore|most}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɔː.məʊst/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-foremost.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊst|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Positioned [[in front of]] (all) others in space, most [[forward]].
#: {{syn|en|front|frontmost|forwardmost}}
#: {{ant|en|back|backmost|hindmost|rear|rearmost|rearwardmost}}
#: {{cot|en|topmost}}
#* {{RQ:Ovid Golding Metamorphosis|book=2|page=22|passage=She [[prank#Verb|prankes]] not by hir mistresse side, she [[presses|preases]] not to bée / The '''foremost''' of the companie, as when she [[erst]] was frée.}}
#* {{RQ:Wells Island of Doctor Moreau|chapter=[https://archive.org/details/islandofdoctormo00welluoft/page/115/mode/1up?q=foremost Chapter 12]|text=As I plunged into the reeds, my '''foremost''' pursuers emerged from the gap.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=w:John Irving|title=w:Avenue of Mysteries|url=https://archive.org/details/avenueofmysterie0000irvi_q3d4/page/70/mode/1up?q=foremost|chapter=7|page=70|publisher=Doubleday|location=London
|passage=Juan Diego hadn’t noticed the other people in the temple, except for what appeared to be two mourners; they knelt in the '''foremost''' pew.}}
# Coming before (all) others in time.
#: {{syn|en|first}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1615|translator=w:George Chapman|title={{w|Homer}}’s {{w|Odyssey|Odysses}}|location=London|publisher=Nathaniel Butter|section=Book 7, p. 102|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A03515.0001.001
|passage={{...}} of both them, she / (By ''{{w|Athena|Pallas}}'' counsell) was to haue the grace / Of '''foremost''' greeting.}}
#* '''1769''', {{w|Oliver Goldsmith}}, ''The Roman History,'' London: S. Baker and G. Leigh ''et al.,'' Volume{{nbsp}}1, Chapter{{nbsp}}16, p.{{nbsp}}254,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004897260.0001.001]</sup>
#*: He was the best horseman, and the swiftest runner of his time. He was ever the '''foremost''' to engage, and the last to retreat;
#* {{ante|1891}}, {{w|Herman Melville}}, ''{{w|Billy Budd}},'' in ''The Shorter Novels of Herman Melville,'' New York: Fawcett Premier, 1956, Chapter{{nbsp}}17, p.{{nbsp}}244,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/shorternovelsofh0000melv_v9g5/page/244/mode/1up?q=foremost]</sup>
#*: a bright young schoolmate of his whom he had seen struck by much the same startling impotence in the act of eagerly rising in the class to be '''foremost''' in response to a testing question put to it by the master
# Of the [[high]]est [[rank]] or [[position]]; of the greatest [[importance]]; of the highest [[priority]].
#: {{syn|en|greatest|leading|paramount|primary|principal|top}}
#: {{ux|en|The exhibition features works by the country’s '''foremost''' artists.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Foremost''' among the workers’ grievances was the company’s failure to address the many safety issues in the plant.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|IV|3|text=What, shall one of us / That struck the '''foremost''' man of all this world / But for supporting robbers, shall we now / Contaminate our fingers with base bribes,}}
#* '''1759''', {{w|George Colman}}, ''The Rolliad,'' Canto{{nbsp}}1, in ''Prose on Several Occasions: Accompanied with Some Pieces in Verse,'' London: T. Cadel, 1787, Volume{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}292,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004896414.0001.002]</sup>
#* '''1846''', {{w|Frederick Douglass}}, Reception Speech at Finsbury Chapel, Moorfields, England, May{{nbsp}}12, 1846, in ''{{w|My Bondage and My Freedom}},'' New York: Miller, Orton & Mulligan, 1855, Appendix, pp.{{nbsp}}410-411,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/mybondagemyfreed00douguoft/page/410/mode/1up?q=foremost]</sup>
#* '''1993''', {{w|Vikram Seth}}, ''{{w|A Suitable Boy}},'' New Delhi: Penguin India, 1994, Section{{nbsp}}9.13, p.{{nbsp}}580,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/suitableboy0000seth/page/580/mode/1up?q=foremost]</sup>
# {{lb|en|nautical}} Closest to the [[bow]].
#: {{ant|en|aftermost}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1900|author=w:Joseph Conrad|title=w:Lord Jim|location=Edinburgh|publisher=William Blackwood|chapter=10|page=133|url=https://archive.org/details/lordjimtale00conrrich/page/133/mode/1up?q=foremost
|passage=I let fall the tiller, turned my back on them, and sat down on the '''foremost''' [[thwart#Noun|thwart]].}}

